\value{
The tag, with the tip's attributes and the engine's dependency added; with a marker, its attributes,
and the marker's own dependency; with \code{when_disabled}, inside a wrapper that holds the reason.
}
\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#stable}{\figure{lifecycle-stable.svg}{options: alt='[Stable]'}}}{\strong{[Stable]}}
Gives one HTML tag a tip that shows after the pointer rests on it, or keyboard focus arrives, and hides when either
leaves. The tip is plain text, or drawn from a named template (\code{\link[=tip_define]{tip_define()}}) filled in from a record: the values of
one item, such as a row of a table. The engine's script and CSS are attached to the tag, so it works in a bslib page,
in a plain Shiny page and in output made later with \code{renderUI()}. A tag inside \code{\link[bslib:tooltip]{bslib::tooltip()}} or
\code{\link[bslib:popover]{bslib::popover()}}, or with \code{data-bs-toggle}, keeps the tip that bslib draws and this one is left off.
}
\details{
A tip shows on keyboard focus as well as on a pointer, so a tag that cannot take focus (a table row, a \code{div}) gives a
keyboard user no way to the tip. \code{tip_on()} looks at the tag and at everything inside it, so the \code{div} that Shiny
puts around an input is fine: the input takes focus. It warns once about a tag with nothing focusable in it, and
never adds a \code{tabindex} itself, because a table of 3,000 rows would become 3,000 Tab stops: give the tags worth a
stop \code{tabindex = "0"}, or put the tip on a button or a link.

A button with no visible words, such as one that holds only an icon, has no name for a screen reader. When it also
has no \code{aria-label} or \code{aria-labelledby} of its own, and the tip is plain \code{text}, \code{tip_on()} gives it an
\code{aria-label} made from the text, with the markdown marks taken out. A tip drawn from a template gives no label,
because its words depend on the record: set \code{aria-label} yourself. The engine reads the tip once: when a tag's
name, from \code{aria-label}, \code{aria-labelledby} or its own words, is the same as the tip's words, the tip is not also
given to a screen reader as a description.

If both \code{text} and \code{template} are given, the text is shown for this tag. A template whose name has been defined in
this session is checked against the record here: a field it names that the record does not hold is an error. A
name that has not been defined is left to the page, which says so once in the browser's console.

A second \code{tip_on()} on a tag that already has a tip replaces the old tip whole: its words, look, \code{id}, \code{events},
\code{pin} and \code{when_disabled} all go, and the new call's arguments are the tip. When the old tip had an \code{id}, \code{events}
or a pin and the new call gives none of them, it warns once, with the class \code{hovercraft_warning_retip}, so a wrapper
that tips a tag again on purpose can silence it with \code{suppressWarnings(classes = )}.

A tag that already has a \code{title} attribute keeps it, and the browser then shows its own tooltip beside the tip.
\code{tip_on()} warns once, with the class \code{hovercraft_warning_title}, and says to remove the \code{title}; it does not
remove it itself, because on an icon it may be the only name.
}
\section{Stable and experimental}{

\code{tag}, \code{text}, \code{template}, \code{record}, \code{placement}, \code{id}, \code{pin} (one pinned tip at a time, with its pin button and
the P key), \code{more} as words or a template (a "More" button opens the long form in the same tip) and a \code{style} that
is a preset name are \verb{r lifecycle::badge("stable")}: their names, values and defaults do not change in 0.1.x. What
the long form's button looks like and the keys beyond the pin's are not part of that promise: the look and place
of the "More" button, the M key and the \code{keys} argument of \code{\link[=use_hovercraft]{use_hovercraft()}} are \verb{r lifecycle::badge("experimental")}, like \code{events} and the fields of the inputs it turns on, \code{when_disabled},
\code{marker}, a \code{style} that is a \code{\link[=tip_style]{tip_style()}}, a \code{\link[=tip_theme]{tip_theme()}} or a bslib theme, a \code{\link[=tip_more]{tip_more()}}, and keeping more
than one pin (\code{max_pins} of \code{\link[=use_hovercraft]{use_hovercraft()}}). They may change.
}

\section{Known limits}{

\itemize{
\item A tag inside \code{\link[bslib:tooltip]{bslib::tooltip()}} or \code{\link[bslib:popover]{bslib::popover()}}, or with \code{data-bs-toggle}, keeps the tip that bslib draws.
\item A pin closes (reason \code{"gone"}) when its item is removed, or hidden by a tab, a conditional panel or a sidebar, and
it does not come back with the item. A pin whose item only scrolls out of view is hidden and returns when the item
does.
\item \code{tip_on()} costs about a millisecond of R time for each tag, and the page's script some time on every change of
the page: for a long table, page it.
\item Reconnecting an app (\code{allowReconnect}) is untested.
}
}

\examples{
tip_on(htmltools::tags$button("Run"), "Runs the **check**")

# The reason a control is switched off
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run", disabled = NA),
  "Runs the **check**",
  when_disabled = "Pick a dataset first"
)

# A tip that a reader can pin, by the button in its corner or by the P key
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run", id = "run"),
  "Runs the **check**",
  id = "run_tip",
  pin = TRUE,
  events = c("hint", "pin")
)

# A short tip with a "More" button that opens the long form in the same tip
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run"),
  "Runs the **check**",
  more = "Checks every column and prints a summary."
)

# The long form shows with the short one
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run"),
  "Runs the **check**",
  more = tip_more("Checks every column and prints a summary.", on = "hover")
)

# A look for this tag only
tip_on(htmltools::tags$button("Run"), "Runs the **check**", style = "sober")
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run"),
  "Runs the **check**",
  style = tip_theme(light = list(bg = "#1b3a6b", text = "#ffffff"))
)

# A visible cue that a word holds a tip
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$span("Baseline", tabindex = "0"),
  "The first **screening** value",
  marker = "underline"
)

tip_define(diff = tip_template(
  tip_title("{{ label }}"),
  tip_text("{{ differences | number:0 }} differences")
))
rows <- data.frame(label = c("ADAE", "ADSL"), differences = c(3, 0))
lapply(seq_len(nrow(rows)), function(i) {
  tip_on(
    htmltools::tags$tr(htmltools::tags$td(rows$label[[i]]), tabindex = "0"),
    template = "diff",
    record = rows[i, ]
  )
})

# The built-in words of the tip, with other words, for this tip
tip_on(
  htmltools::tags$button("Run"),
  "Runs the **check**",
  pin = TRUE,
  id = "run",
  labels = c(close = "Dismiss")
)

# An icon-only button is named from its tip
tip_on(htmltools::tags$button(htmltools::tags$i(class = "icon-gear")), "Open the **settings**")
}
